WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.835
After leaving the station, Phileas Fogg quietly returned to his house on Savile Row.

2
00:00:15.836 --> 00:00:19.802
His neighbors would have been surprised to learn that he had returned.

3
00:00:19.803 --> 00:00:27.795
The doors and windows of his house remained closed. The building was quiet.

4
00:00:27.796 --> 00:00:33.187
Mr. Fogg was ruined! And by the blundering of a detective!

5
00:00:33.188 --> 00:00:37.510
Only a few pounds were left in the carpetbag that had traveled the world with him.

6
00:00:37.511 --> 00:00:44.720
All that remained of his fortune–twenty-thousand pounds deposited at the bank–he now owed to his friends at the Reform Club.

7
00:00:44.721 --> 00:00:50.836
He set aside a room in the house for Aouda, who was overwhelmed with grief at her protector's misfortune.

8
00:00:50.837 --> 00:00:54.546
Passepartout was also desperately concerned for his master.

9
00:00:54.547 --> 00:00:59.030
He watched all evening as Mr. Fogg sat silently in his armchair.

10
00:00:59.031 --> 00:01:02.992
Late at night Mr. Fogg climbed the stairs to bed.

11
00:01:02.993 --> 00:01:08.509
Many a ruined man has chosen suicide, and fearful that Mr. Fogg planned some desperate act,

12
00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:12.445
Passepartout waited faithfully all night at his master's door.

13
00:01:12.446 --> 00:01:18.713
In the morning Mr. Fogg called Passepartout into his room and asked him to look after Aouda's breakfast and lunch.

14
00:01:18.714 --> 00:01:21.887
Mr. Fogg would be too busy setting his affairs in order.

15
00:01:21.888 --> 00:01:26.872
In the evening he would ask permission to have a few moments of conversation with the lady.

16
00:01:26.873 --> 00:01:31.831
Passepartout felt so horrible and responsible that he could contain himself no longer.

17
00:01:31.832 --> 00:01:38.110
"My master! Mr. Fogg!" he cried. "Don't you blame me? It was my fault that—"

18
00:01:38.111 --> 00:01:42.874
"I blame no one," returned Mr. Fogg. "Go!"

19
00:01:42.875 --> 00:01:48.316
Passepartout left Mr. Fogg's room and went to deliver the master's message to Aouda.

20
00:01:48.317 --> 00:01:52.715
Mr. Fogg shut himself in his room and busied himself at his desk.

21
00:01:52.716 --> 00:01:55.553
Passepartout also had a matter to settle.

22
00:01:55.554 --> 00:01:59.488
In the mailbox that morning, he had found a bill from the gas company.

23
00:01:59.489 --> 00:02:03.850
He remembered at once that the heater in his room had been burning for eighty days!

24
00:02:03.851 --> 00:02:09.650
He wasted not one minute more and rushed to his room to shut it off.

25
00:02:09.651 --> 00:02:14.104
At half-past seven that evening, Mr. Fogg knocked on Aouda's door.

26
00:02:14.105 --> 00:02:16.901
No emotion was visible on his face.

27
00:02:16.902 --> 00:02:20.129
He entered and sat several minutes without speaking.

28
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:24.849
Then he said, "Madam, please forgive me for bringing you to England.

29
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:29.852
When I decided to bring you so far away from your country, I was rich.

30
00:02:29.853 --> 00:02:33.483
I was going to put a portion of my fortune at your disposal.

31
00:02:33.484 --> 00:02:37.974
Then you would have been free and happy. But now I am ruined."

32
00:02:37.975 --> 00:02:44.801
"I know it, Mr. Fogg," said Aouda. "And I ask in return, will you forgive me for following you?

33
00:02:44.802 --> 00:02:48.985
For, perhaps, delaying you and contributing to your loss."

34
00:02:48.986 --> 00:02:52.261
"Madam, you could not have remained in India.

35
00:02:52.262 --> 00:02:57.421
Your safety depended on traveling as far as possible from those chasing you."

36
00:02:57.422 --> 00:03:00.386
"But what will become of you?" asked Aouda.

37
00:03:00.387 --> 00:03:03.224
"I have need of nothing," said Fogg.

38
00:03:03.225 --> 00:03:09.332
"Surely poverty will not overtake a man like you," said Aouda. "Your friends—"

39
00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:12.889
"They are not close friends," he replied simply.

40
00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.572
"Your relatives—"

41
00:03:14.573 --> 00:03:17.312
"I no longer have any relatives."

42
00:03:17.313 --> 00:03:23.012
"Mr. Fogg," said Aouda, rising. "May I offer you a kinswoman and a friend?

43
00:03:23.013 --> 00:03:26.286
Will you have me for your wife?"

44
00:03:26.287 --> 00:03:29.084
At this, Mr. Fogg rose in his turn.

45
00:03:29.085 --> 00:03:34.857
There was a light in his eyes and a slight trembling of his lips. Aouda looked into his face.

46
00:03:34.858 --> 00:03:40.870
Fogg shut his eyes for an instant as if to avoid her look, but when he opened them again, he said, "I love you.

47
00:03:40.871 --> 00:03:46.074
Yes, by all that is holiest, I love you, and I am entirely yours."

48
00:03:46.075 --> 00:03:50.835
"Ah!" cried Aouda, pressing his hand to her heart.

49
00:03:50.836 --> 00:03:53.823
Passepartout was summoned and appeared immediately.

50
00:03:53.824 --> 00:03:59.958
Mr. Fogg still held Aouda's hand in his own, and Passepartout immediately understood what had happened.

51
00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:04.290
Passepartout's face became as radiant as the tropical sun at its highest.

52
00:04:04.291 --> 00:04:10.500
Mr. Fogg asked him if it was not too late to notify the Reverend Samuel Wilson that very evening.

53
00:04:10.501 --> 00:04:15.842
Passepartout smiled one of his warmest smiles and said, "It is never too late!"

54
00:04:15.843 --> 00:04:19.249
It was five minutes past eight in the evening.

55
00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.434
"Will the wedding be tomorrow?" asked Passepartout.

56
00:04:22.435 --> 00:04:26.801
"Tomorrow," said Mr. Fogg, turning to Aouda. "Monday."

57
00:04:26.802 --> 00:04:29.975
"Yes, Monday," she replied.

58
00:04:29.976 --> 00:04:39.722
Passepartout hurried off as fast as his legs could carry him.

59
00:04:39.723 --> 00:04:41.903
At least something good came out of our trip.

60
00:04:41.904 --> 00:04:45.490
Mr. Fogg and Aouda are in love and will get married!

61
00:04:45.491 --> 00:04:50.395
I must find Reverend Wilson and start preparing for the wedding. There is so much to do!

62
00:04:50.396 --> 00:04:53.160
Did you know that most brides in England wear white?

63
00:04:53.161 --> 00:04:59.129
This tradition was started when Queen Victoria got married in 1840 and wore a white dress.

64
00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:03.858
Before that brides wore just about any color, with red being quite a popular choice.

65
00:05:03.859 --> 00:05:07.239
As for Mr. Fogg, well, he won't be wearing white!

66
00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:12.163
He might wear a frock coat, the long dress coat that is traditional for Victorian gentlemen.

67
00:05:12.164 --> 00:05:15.529
But today many men are wearing morning coats to weddings.

68
00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:19.520
A morning coat is cut short in the front with two long tails in the back.

69
00:05:19.521 --> 00:05:22.140
A morning coat is black or gray.

70
00:05:22.141 --> 00:05:26.138
After the church wedding, it is customary to have a wedding breakfast.

71
00:05:26.139 --> 00:05:33.236
I must be sure to reserve a special corner in Mr. Fogg's house where Aouda can receive the guests who come to congratulate the happy couple.

72
00:05:33.237 --> 00:05:37.065
After the reception, perhaps Mr. Fogg and Aouda will go on a honeymoon.

73
00:05:37.066 --> 00:05:39.646
Please forgive me, readers, if I do not linger much longer.

74
00:05:39.647 --> 00:05:46.022
Why, I have a wedding to plan!
